{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>Please do not leave us</b> etc. According to the words of Rabbi Eliezer it is explained in this manner: he asks of him that he not depart even for an hour, although his intention is to return, and this is what he alluded to in the word ״נָא״. And corresponding to the argument of Shraga in the Tihra (Jethro) who did not accept it from him, he stated the reason <b>for therefore you know our encampments in the wilderness</b> — and through you our ways were illuminated by your counsel, as it is written (Exodus 18:21) ״וְאַתָּה תֶחֱזֶה מִכָּל הָעָם״ etc., and HaShem agreed to your counsel, and it is written ״וַיִּבְחַר מֹשֶׁה״ etc.; if so, you are able to illuminate even before the sun and the moon." "And according to the words of Rabbi Joshua it is explained in this manner: <b>Please do not</b> — meaning, it is not fitting now to leave us. And the reason — <b>for therefore you know</b> etc. — meaning: granted, if you had not come to us at all, I would not be concerned about you; but now that you have come and you know our encampments in the wilderness — meaning, because Israel are covered by clouds and no one knows their place, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah ch. 19, Rosh Hashanah 3a) on the verse ״כִּי גָוַע אַהֲרֹן״ etc. ״וַיִּשְׁמַע הַכְּנַעֲנִי כִּי בָּא יִשְׂרָאֵל״ etc. — and our Sages of blessed memory said that because the clouds of glory departed, the place of Israel was recognized; say from this point on: beforehand their encampments were not known, and Hobab — HaShem informed him of the encampments of Israel. Moreover, HaShem agreed to that by which he illuminated their eyes through his counsel, which is His saying <b>and you shall be to us</b> etc. — and it is not fitting to leave them after all this, for there is in this a profanation of the Name, for they would say that he stood by their faith and they held him in such esteem and he abandoned them — this is only because the signs of the faith were not vindicated in his eyes, and it is not fitting to do so. And when he did not accept, Moses removed him from the honor of the world."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "״שרגא בטיהרא״ is Aramaic for Jethro (Yitro); I have left it in transliteration as Or HaChaim cites it from rabbinic idiom."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:30.162786799Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}